    "lastCommit": {
      "id": "c0e4e7dabbc97acb36e39a046a551b9ea2e00423",
      "message": "OTA-1887 \u2014 the copy speaks for itself (GOLEM ONLY)\n\nDirect continuation of OTA-1886: the owner ran PRESERVE LEGACY SAVE\nDATABASE on the actual Golem device and it found RKStorage (20,480 bytes)\nplus a 0-byte RKStorage-journal, preserved with a verified byte-identical\ncopy. This turn answers what is actually inside that preserved copy,\nwithout ever touching the original again.\n\nNeither expo-sqlite nor any other SQLite-capable module is a dependency\nof this project (checked: not in package.json, node_modules, or any\napp.json config plugin). The only SQLite engine compiled into this build\nlives entirely inside @react-native-async-storage/async-storage's own\nJava code and is not exposed to JS as an \"open an arbitrary file as a\ndatabase\" API. So there is no already-linked, OTA-safe native path to\ncross this boundary; adding one would mean a new native build, which this\ndiagnostic avoids unless separately authorized. The alternative that\nstays inside OTA JS is to decode the documented SQLite file format by\nhand, the same way OTA-1886 wrote its own SHA-256 rather than add\nexpo-crypto.\n\nAdds app/diagnostics/legacyStorageInspect.ts\n(runLegacyStorageInspection / formatLegacyStorageInspectReport) and a\n\"READ PRESERVED LEGACY SAVE\" button beside OTA-1886's in the About\nscreen's DIAGNOSTIC TOOLS drawer. It never references the original\ndatabase's location at all: it only reads OTA-1886's own pristine\npreservation copy, copies THAT into a second, disposable working copy,\nhash-verifies the working copy against the pristine one, and only then\ndecodes the working copy's bytes as SQLite (file header, one table\nb-tree walk with interior/leaf/overflow-page support, record decoding).\nThe pristine copy and the original are never written to again.\n\nThe reader's correctness is checked in\nota1887LegacyStorageInspectIsReadOnly against bytes a REAL SQLite engine\nproduced (python's sqlite3, not hand-guessed fixtures): an empty table, a\nsmall realistic key set (including a full Tartaria save-shaped record), a\nvalue large enough to force SQLite's own overflow pages (12KB+, verified\nbyte-for-byte reconstructed), a database with no save table at all, and\n400 rows forced across a genuinely multi-leaf b-tree (proving the\ninterior-page traversal, not just a single leaf). 46/46 passing,\nincluding static proof that the module's source never references\n/data/data/ or a databases directory, never calls execSQL/PRAGMA/\nexpo-sqlite, and never reaches any AsyncStorage-mutating or save-repair\npath.\n\nBOUNDED DISCLOSURE, NOT A FULL DUMP: every key name found is reported,\nbut only recognized Tartaria save keys (tartaria.slot.<id>.v2[.bak]) have\ntheir values parsed, and only into the same short identity fields\nOTA-1885's probe and saveSnapshot.ts's HIGHLIGHTS line already use (name,\nrace, faction, hp, dead, hours elapsed, location, main-quest phase,\nGuardians defeated, saved-at) \u2014 never the full save JSON. No restoration,\nmigration, or character construction is attempted. NOT PUBLISHED TO HAL \u2014\nGolem only.\n\nIsolated npm run test:ci:fast: 1426/1426 suites, 16550/16553 tests (3\nskipped, 0 failed), exit 0. typecheck:ci clean. typecheck:tests at\nbaseline (196, no growth). lint clean. Store ceiling unchanged\n(gameStore.ts untouched).\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01LGFtzR6AQJbu4XdR28y3fX",
